---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Comments from '../../components/Comments.astro';
const P = '/misc/hotels/';
---
<BaseLayout title="Rooms with a View — Huanyu Chen"
  description="The hotels and resorts we liked most from five years of trips, from a Mauna Lani atrium and one hotel a night in Waikīkī to high floors over Times Square, the Strip, Dallas, Chicago and Sydney.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · 2022–2026</p>
  <h1>Rooms with a View</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">The hotels we would happily stay at again, and what we saw from them.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>25 hotels · 2022–2026</strong><br />
    5 hotels in 5 nights in Waikīkī · 7 resorts · 12 city hotels, most of them high up · 1 cabin in the woods<br />
    Highest room: the 43rd floor in Sydney · best New Year's Eve: right above Times Square
  </div>

  <p>
    On 小红书, people share the hotels they stayed at the way others share restaurants: the room, the view, the
    pool, and whether it was worth it. Going through five years of photos for the other posts on this page, I found
    that I had been doing half of that without noticing. Almost every trip has a few photos taken from a hotel
    window, usually on the first evening, before unpacking.
  </p>
  <p>
    So here are the hotels we liked, out of the eighty or so we have slept in since 2022. Most of the rest were
    one-night stops on long drives, the kind of place you remember only for its waffle machine. These are the
    ones worth a detour, sorted by region, each with a map. At a resort the reason to stay was usually the
    balcony; in a city, the room I want is the one on the highest floor I can get. None of this is
    sponsored; quite a few of the city nights were booked with hotel points.
  </p>

  <p class="toc"><strong>By region:</strong> <a href="#waikiki">Waikīkī</a> · <a href="#big-island-kauai">Big Island and Kauaʻi</a> ·
    <a href="#california">California</a> · <a href="#las-vegas-sedona">Las Vegas and Sedona</a> ·
    <a href="#texas-colorado">Texas and Colorado</a> · <a href="#new-york-chicago">New York and Chicago</a> ·
    <a href="#abroad">Outside the US</a></p>

  <h2 id="waikiki">Waikīkī, one hotel a night</h2>
  <p>
    On Oʻahu in September 2026 we tried something we had never done before: a different hotel every night. Waikīkī
    is small, and it turned five nights into a tasting menu.
    Here they are in the order I would recommend them.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'map-oahu.webp'} alt="Street map of Waikīkī marking five hotels with photo pins: 1 Ka Laʻi Waikiki Beach, 2 Hilton Hawaiian Village, 3 Ambassador Waikiki, 4 Embassy Suites Waikiki Beach Walk, 5 DoubleTree by Hilton Alana." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Hotels in Waikīkī: 1 Ka Laʻi Waikiki Beach, 2 Hilton Hawaiian Village, 3 Ambassador Waikiki, 4 Embassy Suites Waikiki Beach Walk, 5 DoubleTree by Hilton Alana. The numbers follow my ranking.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>1. Ka Laʻi Waikiki Beach, LXR</h3>
  <p>
    The best of the five, and the quietest. Our balcony faced west over the green lawns of Fort DeRussy, so the
    sunset dropped straight into the ocean in front of us, with the towers of Waikīkī lined up along the shore. At
    7:45 that Friday evening, the balcony gave us front-row seats for the fireworks from Hilton Hawaiian Village's
    luau, bursting over the beach next door.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kalai-fireworks.webp'} alt="Fireworks bursting red, green and gold over the beach at night, with pink fireworks rising from the shore, a lit boat on the water and hotel towers at the right." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Friday fireworks from the balcony, 7:45 pm (a frame from a video).</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Before that came the sunset, and in the morning the same view was all park, palms and blue water.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kalai-sunset.webp'} alt="Sunset over the ocean from a glass-railed balcony, with Waikīkī hotel towers along the shore." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sunset from the balcony, 6:24 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kalai-morning.webp'} alt="Morning view over the green park of Fort DeRussy to hotel towers and the ocean." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The next morning, over Fort DeRussy.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    There is also an infinity pool above the palms, and the bathroom has a deep marble soaking tub.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kalai-pool.webp'} alt="The edge of an infinity pool with a glass railing, palms and treetops beyond, and a tower at the left." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The pool, 9:31 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'kalai-tub.webp'} alt="A deep white soaking tub set in gray-veined marble, with a wooden bath tray and rolled towels." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The soaking tub.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>2. Hilton Hawaiian Village</h3>
  <p>
    The classic. It is a whole village of towers at the west end of the beach, with a lagoon and pools, and our
    room was high enough to see the full curve of Waikīkī Beach all the way to Diamond Head.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hhv-panorama.webp'} alt="High view over Waikīkī: hotel towers, pools, the beach curving east and Diamond Head on the horizon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The whole beach, and Diamond Head.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hhv-balcony.webp'} alt="A shaded balcony with two chairs, framing the towers, the curve of the beach, turquoise water and Diamond Head." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The balcony, in the morning.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>3. Ambassador Waikiki</h3>
  <p>
    The surprise. The Ambassador is a small Tapestry hotel a few blocks back from the beach, and our room had a
    double-decker bed: compact, cozy and a bit like a college dorm. My wife liked it a lot, more than some of the
    much grander rooms on this list. We did not take a single photo there, so the pictures below are the hotel's own.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ambassador-bunk-room.webp'} alt="A compact hotel room: a bed below a wooden bunk with a ladder, a window onto city towers, a wall-mounted TV, two round leather stools and a slatted wooden wardrobe." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The bunk-bed room.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ambassador-bunk-close.webp'} alt="Close-up of the wooden bunk bed with white bedding, a ladder and a reading light, beside a window and pink leaf-patterned wallpaper." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Bunk, ladder and reading light. Photos: Ambassador Waikiki (hotel website).</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>4. Embassy Suites Waikiki Beach Walk</h3>
  <p>
    A solid suite in the middle of everything, and the best sunrise of the trip: at 6:36 on our last morning the
    clouds over the rooftops turned gold.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'embassy-waikiki-dawn.webp'} alt="Dawn over Waikīkī rooftops and towers from a balcony, with gold clouds above." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Dawn from the balcony.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>5. DoubleTree by Hilton Alana</h3>
  <p>
    Last on my list, but not for the view. Our room was high up, facing inland over central Waikīkī rather than the
    sea, and at night the towers around it were lit window by window, a vertical city of tiny yellow rooms, with
    the streets and pool decks glowing far below. It was a great view.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'alana-towers.webp'} alt="Waikīkī towers lit up at night, with a street and a glowing pool far below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>From the room, 9:24 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'alana-night.webp'} alt="Night view from a balcony over lit hotel towers and a pool deck below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The same balcony, a few seconds later.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2 id="big-island-kauai">The Big Island and Kauaʻi</h2>
  <p class="region-lead">Three resorts: two on the Big Island's Kohala Coast and one on Kauaʻi's north shore.</p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'map-big-island-kauai.webp'} alt="Map of the Hawaiian Islands marking three resorts: 1 Mauna Lani and 3 Hilton Waikoloa Village on the Big Island, 2 Westin Princeville on Kauaʻi." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Hotels on the Big Island and Kauaʻi: 1 Mauna Lani, 2 Westin Princeville Ocean Resort Villas, 3 Hilton Waikoloa Village.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>1. Mauna Lani, Auberge Resorts · Big Island, 2025</h3>
  <p>
    The most beautiful building on this list. The hotel is built around a huge open-air atrium filled with a
    garden, and our ocean-view balcony looked straight down the beach and over the palms to the sea. I wrote about
    the design in the <a href="/misc/big-island-2025">Big Island post</a>; I would recommend it to anyone visiting
    the Kohala Coast.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'maunalani-balcony.webp'} alt="An ocean-view balcony framed by an overhang: blue sea, a beach and palms." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The balcony, 11 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'maunalani-atrium.webp'} alt="Looking down into the open-air atrium: terraced balconies, palms and a wooden walkway." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The atrium.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>2. Westin Princeville Ocean Resort Villas · Kauaʻi, 2026</h3>
  <p>
    On Kauaʻi's north shore, a lagoon-style pool under enormous clouds and a stream full of koi below a small
    waterfall.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'princeville-lagoon-pool.webp'} alt="A still lagoon-style pool reflecting palms and white clouds, with thatched cabanas and loungers beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The pool, 8:21 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'princeville-koi.webp'} alt="Orange and white koi in a stream below a rock waterfall and a lawn." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The koi stream.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>3. Hilton Waikoloa Village · Big Island, 2026</h3>
  <p>
    A resort big enough to need a tram and a canal boat, with a gallery of statues along its walkways and a
    balcony that looked over lawns and lagoons to the ocean. One morning a bright green gecko sat on the balcony wall.
    At night the lagoons light up. More in the
    <a href="/misc/hawaii-2026">Hawaiʻi 2026 post</a>.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'waikoloa-balcony.webp'} alt="Balcony view over landscaped lawns and lagoons to the ocean on a sunny morning." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The view, 10 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'waikoloa-night.webp'} alt="Night view from a balcony over the lit resort grounds and lagoons." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The same view after midnight.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2 id="california">California</h2>
  <p class="region-lead">From San Francisco down to Catalina, with Oxnard and the pines near Big Bear in between.</p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'map-california.webp'} alt="Map of California marking four hotels: 1 Hyatt Regency San Francisco, 2 Hotel St. Lauren on Catalina, 3 Zachari Dunes in Oxnard, 4 Postcard Cabins near Big Bear." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Hotels in California: 1 Hyatt Regency San Francisco, 2 Hotel St. Lauren, 3 Zachari Dunes on Mandalay Beach, 4 Postcard Cabins.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>1. Hyatt Regency · San Francisco</h3>
  <p>
    This one earns a mention for something other than the view: the Hyatt Regency San Francisco's tiered atrium,
    seen from the top floors at midnight.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hyattsf-atrium.webp'} alt="Looking down a tall tiered hotel atrium onto a lit bar and a large sculpture." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Hyatt Regency San Francisco.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>2. Hotel St. Lauren · Catalina, 2024</h3>
  <p>
    A smaller place. The St. Lauren sits on the hill above Avalon, and its balconies look over the town's
    rooftops to the harbor.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'stlauren-harbor.webp'} alt="Avalon from the hill: white buildings and palms above the harbor, with a cruise ship anchored offshore." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Avalon and the harbor from the hill.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>3. Zachari Dunes on Mandalay Beach · Oxnard, 2025</h3>
  <p>
    The easiest escape from Los Angeles on this list: an hour up the coast, a Spanish-style courtyard with a rock
    waterfall, and a balcony over red tile roofs to the dunes and the ocean.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'zachari-balcony.webp'} alt="View from a balcony over red tile roof to sand dunes, palms and the ocean." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>From the balcony, 8:37 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'zachari-courtyard.webp'} alt="A courtyard path with succulent beds and a rock waterfall between red-roofed wings." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The courtyard.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>4. Postcard Cabins · Big Bear</h3>
  <p>
    A tiny black cabin in the pines with a big picture window over the bed. Our
    dog came too.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'postcard-cabin.webp'} alt="A small black cabin with a big picture window, among pine trees." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Postcard Cabins, Big Bear.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2 id="las-vegas-sedona">Las Vegas and Sedona</h2>
  <p class="region-lead">Three hotels on the Strip, and one resort among Sedona's red rocks.</p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'map-southwest.webp'} alt="Map of southern Nevada and northern Arizona marking four hotels: 1 The Palazzo, 2 ARIA and 3 Caesars Palace in Las Vegas, 4 The Wilde in Sedona." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Hotels in Las Vegas and Sedona: 1 The Palazzo, 2 ARIA, 3 Caesars Palace, 4 The Wilde.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>1. The Palazzo · Las Vegas, 2022 and 2024</h3>
  <p>
    Las Vegas rooms are mostly about the view out of them. The Palazzo gave us a suite in 2022, with the Venetian
    tower and the Strip below the window.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'palazzo-view.webp'} alt="High view down onto the Venetian tower and the Strip in daylight." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Palazzo, 2022: the Venetian below.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'palazzo-suite.webp'} alt="A suite living room with dark red curtains, a round table, a desk and a sofa in front of a large window." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The suite.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    In 2024 we were back at the Palazzo for a night, in a room facing the Sphere.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'palazzo-sphere.webp'} alt="The Sphere lit up at night, seen from a high hotel room, with the city lights behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Palazzo, 2024: the Sphere at midnight.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>2. ARIA · Las Vegas</h3>
  <p>
    At ARIA, a high floor looked straight at Vdara next door and down onto the pool deck.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'aria-vdara.webp'} alt="The curved blue glass Vdara tower beside the city and mountains, seen from a high room." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>ARIA: Vdara next door.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'aria-pool.webp'} alt="Looking straight down onto a pool deck with teal umbrellas and a curved entry drive." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>ARIA's pool, from above.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>3. Caesars Palace · Las Vegas, 2024</h3>
  <p>
    The night after that second Palazzo stay we were at Caesars Palace, where the Garden of the Gods pools look
    like a Roman ruin with sun loungers.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'caesars-pools.webp'} alt="High view over the Caesars Palace pools: a domed temple, round pools, red loungers and statues." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Caesars Palace: the Garden of the Gods.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>4. The Wilde · Sedona, 2026</h3>
  <p>
    A smaller place. The Wilde, in Sedona, is a resort of stucco wings and terraced red-walled gardens with
    the red rocks behind.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wilde-garden.webp'} alt="Terraced red-walled garden stairs with desert plants beside a stucco wing." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The gardens at the Wilde.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2 id="texas-colorado">Texas and Colorado</h2>
  <p class="region-lead">Dallas and Austin, and two hotels in downtown Denver.</p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'map-texas-colorado.webp'} alt="Map of the central United States marking four hotels: 1 Hyatt Regency Dallas, 2 Four Seasons Austin, 3 Embassy Suites Denver, 4 Art Hotel Denver." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Hotels in Texas and Colorado: 1 Hyatt Regency Dallas, 2 Four Seasons Austin, 3 Embassy Suites Denver, 4 The Art Hotel Denver.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>1. Hyatt Regency Dallas · 2023</h3>
  <p>
    Next to Reunion Tower, and the view of the downtown skyline is the best thing about it, especially at night,
    when one tower is outlined in green.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'dallas-night.webp'} alt="Dallas skyline at night with a tower outlined in green lights." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Night, 9:37 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'dallas-morning.webp'} alt="Downtown Dallas by day from a high room: Bank of America Plaza, green-glass towers and the red Old Red courthouse, with rail lines below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The next morning.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>2. Four Seasons · Austin, 2023</h3>
  <p>
    Another one that earns a mention for something other than the view: the Four Seasons Austin's quiet courtyard
    of old trees.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'fsaustin-courtyard.webp'} alt="A courtyard of large trees below hotel towers in Austin." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Four Seasons Austin.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>3. Embassy Suites Denver · 2024</h3>
  <p>
    A conference hotel with a good window: the Embassy Suites by the convention center had the downtown
    towers.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'denver-embassy.webp'} alt="Downtown Denver towers and rooftops in morning light." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Embassy Suites Denver, 2024.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>4. The Art Hotel Denver · 2026</h3>
  <p>
    Two years later, on a spring-break drive, the Art Hotel Denver looked over the art museum's roof garden to the
    Rocky Mountains.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'arthotel-mountains.webp'} alt="A museum roof garden and city buildings with the Rocky Mountains on the horizon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Art Hotel Denver, 2026.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2 id="new-york-chicago">New York and Chicago</h2>
  <p class="region-lead">Times Square from above, and the Chicago skyline across the water.</p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'map-new-york-chicago.webp'} alt="Map of the Great Lakes and the Northeast marking two hotels: 1 Hotel Edison in New York, 2 Sable at Navy Pier in Chicago." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Hotels in New York and Chicago: 1 Hotel Edison, 2 Sable at Navy Pier.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>1. Hotel Edison · New York, New Year's Eve 2022</h3>
  <p>
    Hard to beat for location. Our window looked straight down on Times Square, so on New Year's Eve we
    watched the crowds, the stage and the billboards from above.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'edison-times-square.webp'} alt="Looking straight down from a hotel window onto the billboards and stage area of Times Square at night." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Times Square from the room, 8:42 pm on December 31.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>2. Sable at Navy Pier · Chicago, 2024</h3>
  <p>
    The one I would recommend most from our <a href="/misc/roadtrip-2024">2024 road trip</a>. Most nights on that
    trip were in budget hotels along the highway, to save money for the drive. For Chicago we redeemed points for
    the Sable, a hotel on Navy Pier itself, and it was worth every one: the window looks back across the water at
    the whole Chicago skyline, with the cruise boats right below. The bathroom had backlit mirrors and a wood-tiled
    shower.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sable-skyline.webp'} alt="The Chicago skyline across the water from Navy Pier, with a cruise boat below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The skyline from the room, 9:07 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sable-bath.webp'} alt="A hotel bathroom with two backlit round-cornered mirrors and a wood-tiled glass shower." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The bathroom.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2 id="abroad">Outside the US</h2>
  <p class="region-lead">Cancún, Sydney and Vancouver.</p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'map-abroad.webp'} alt="Map of the Pacific marking three hotels: 1 Hyatt Ziva Cancún, 2 Hilton Sydney, 3 Hilton Vancouver Downtown." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Hotels outside the US: 1 Hyatt Ziva Cancún, 2 Hilton Sydney, 3 Hilton Vancouver Downtown.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>1. Hyatt Ziva Cancún · 2022</h3>
  <p>
    An all-inclusive at the tip of the Cancún hotel zone. The room looked down on
    a stack of blue pools, a palapa and the Caribbean, and the infinity pools by the lobby seem to run straight
    into the sea.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ziva-room-view.webp'} alt="High view over blue pools, a lawn and a palapa to the Caribbean." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>From the room.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ziva-infinity.webp'} alt="Infinity pools under a canopy, with palms and the sea beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The infinity pools.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>2. Hilton Sydney · 2023</h3>
  <p>
    The highest room on this list: the 43rd floor, in the middle of the city. At sunset the towers lit up below us,
    with the green dome of the Queen Victoria Building straight down, and at six in the morning there was a window seat to sit in and watch them wake up. (The four nights before
    that, at the Novotel, were less memorable; my advisor, at the Sofitel next door, had a stunning view of the
    harbor.)
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sydney-qvb.webp'} alt="Looking straight down from a high window on city towers at sunset, with the green copper dome of the Queen Victoria Building at the bottom." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Straight down from the 43rd floor at sunset.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sydney-nook.webp'} alt="A deep window seat framing city towers in morning light." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The window seat, 6:39 am.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>3. Hilton Vancouver Downtown · 2025</h3>
  <p>
    A conference hotel with a good window: the Hilton downtown looked down a street of glass
    towers to the white roof of BC Place.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'vancouver-bcplace.webp'} alt="High view over Vancouver towers to the white roof of BC Place." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Hilton Vancouver Downtown, 2025.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>What I look for now</h2>
  <p>
    After eighty-odd hotels, my rule is simple: a high floor in a city, a balcony at a resort, and a window I want
    to photograph before I unpack. The rooms blur together after a while; the photos from the windows don't.
  </p>
  <p class="muted"><small>Hotels listed from my own photos, booking records and notes; times are local. Photo
    locations have been removed from the published images. No hotel paid for or asked for this post.</small></p>
  <p class="muted"><small>Maps: © OpenStreetMap contributors; hotel locations are approximate.</small></p>
  <p class="muted"><small>Some photos in this post are my wife's.</small></p>
